cs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L97:   * cite43†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L98:   * cite44†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L99: \nL100: Company\nL101:   * cite45†About Us L102:   * cite46†Our Charter L103:   * cite47†Careers L104:   * cite9†News L105: \nL106: Support\nL107: \nL108:   * cite48†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L109: \nL110: More\nL111: \nL112:   * cite49†Stories L113:   * cite50†Academy L114:   * cite51†Supply Co. L115:   * cite52†Livestreams L116:   * cite53†Podcast L117:   * cite54†RSS L118: \nL119: Terms & Policies\nL120: \nL121:   * cite55†Terms of Use L122:   * cite56†Privacy Policy L123:   * cite57†Other Policies L124: cite58†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L125: \nL126: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL127: \nL128: English United States\nL129: \nL130: [Input: Search]\nL131: ### Dots\nL132: \nL133: Dots are remarkably capable, always-on agents built to handle everything. They’re a whole new way to work with AI—one that gets to know what matters to you, is always working on your behalf, and takes important work off your plate so you get more of your time and attention back.\nL134: \nL135: Available on Pro and Business Premium in eligible markets. Enterprise, Edu, and Healthcare users can try the beta when their workspace admin enables it; it is off by default. cite10†Learn more⁠ L136: ### GPT-6.1 Sol\nL137: \nL138: We’re introducing GPT‑6.1 Sol, a major upgrade to GPT‑6 Sol with exceptionally strong performance on agentic coding, along with computer use, and professional work. It delivers near-Astra intelligence to everyone at a fifth of its standard input and output token prices, giving developers more flexibility to execute complex workflows within the same budget.\nL139: \nL140: Available to all API, Plus, Pro, Business, Enterprise, and Edu users. cite16†Learn more⁠ L141: ### Ultrafast\nL142: \nL143: Ultrafast is our premium speed tier for workloads where speed matters most. Ultrafast offers up to 8× faster token generation (300 tokens per second) in Codex and up to 6x in the API.\nL144: \nL145: GPT‑6 Astra Ultrafast is available today in the cite65†API⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com and in cite66†ChatGPT Work and Codex⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com on Pro 500 and Enterprise plans. GPT‑6.1 Sol Ultrafast is coming soon.\nL146: ### Private Intelligence\nL147: \nL148: OpenAI Private Intelligence helps businesses use frontier AI with greater confidence that their data is protected. cite67†Zero Data Retention with Private Safety Processing⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com enables automated safety reviews without giving OpenAI personnel access to the underlying content. Our preview of Private Inference, coming this fall, combines confidential computing with strict, verifiable controls.\nL149: \nL150: cite68†Contact us⁠ about Private Intelligence\nL151: ### Codex in the cloud\nL152: \nL153: Now developers can run Codex wherever they need it: on a computer, remotely from a phone, or in the cloud from any device. Reusable development environments help tasks start quickly and give your team a shared setup with approved settings and permissions.\nL154: \nL155: Available on Plus, Pro, Business, Healthcare, Education, and Enterprise. cite69†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com L156: ### A refreshed Codex CLI\nL157: \nL158: The Codex CLI now lets you start and steer tasks with your voice. The new /agents view makes it easier to delegate work and track multiple tasks at once. We’ve also improved everyday workflows to help  you edit prompts, resume sessions, and use worktrees, while a cleaner terminal UI makes longer sessions easier to read.\nL159: \nL160: Available to all plans. cite70†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com L161: ### Code Review\nL162: \nL163: The new code review experience in the ChatGPT desktop app makes it easier to review changes across projects. You can read summaries, explore diffs, and ask Codex about potential issues before sharing feedback on GitHub pull requests or GitLab merge requests. With automatic reviews, Codex can also take a first pass in the cloud while you’re away.\nL164: \nL165: Available on all plans. cite71†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com L166: ### Codex Security Cloud\nL167: \nL168: Codex Security Cloud gives defenders a better set of tools to harden their infrastructure. Scan entire GitHub repositories on demand or on a schedule, with ongoing checks of new commits. Codex investigates findings, removes duplicates and prepares fixes in the cloud, even with your laptop closed. It includes access to models offered through cite72†Daybreak Blue⁠ without a separate Daybreak application.\nL169: Available to all Pro, Business, Enterprise and Edu users on desktop and web. cite73†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com L170: ### Decisions API\nL171: \nL172: Decisions API enables real-time decision-making by focusing Luna's intelligence on a specific set of user-defined questions with finite pre-defined answers. Developers supply context using text or images, and get back answers they can use to classify content, route requests, or choose an agent’s next action.\nL173: \nL174: Available in limited preview today with a broad release planned in the coming days.\nL175: ### Agents API with Computer use\nL176: \nL177: The cite74†Agents API⁠ now supports computer use so developers can build agents that interact with software to complete tasks. It also brings Codex’s multi-agent capabilities, tool search, tool calling, and context compaction into your application. OpenAI runs the underlying infrastructure so your team can focus on building the application.\nL178: Available through the API and in Codex and ChatGPT Work on Pro 500 and Enterprise. cite75†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L179: ### Bedrock Managed Agents, powered by OpenAI\nL180: \nL181: OpenAI worked with Amazon on Bedrock Managed Agents to take the core capabilities of the Agents API and add customization to work natively in AWS and integrate with AWS resources. Now you can use Bedrock Managed Agents to build OpenAI agents that run entirely in AWS. cite76†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†aws.amazon.com L182: ### Plugin extensions\nL183: \nL184: We’re opening the platform we use to build ChatGPT features so developers can create their own experiences within ChatGPT. Plugin extensions let you give your plugin a home in the sidebar and build interactive panels where people can work alongside the conversation. You can also create viewers for the file types your product supports.\nL185: \nL186: Available to all plans. cite77†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L187: ### Improved plugin creation, submission, and discovery\nL188: \nL189: We’re making it easier to build plugins and help people discover them. Plugin Creator helps you build your plugin while a redesigned submission flow provides clearer feedback. Improved ranking and recommendations help people find relevant plugins in the directory and in conversations. Users choose which plugins to use and approve the access each one receives.\nL190: \nL191: Available to all plans. cite78†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L192: ### Sites can now host plugins\nL193: \nL194: You can now add supported ChatGPT plugins to the Sites you build. Teammates in your workspace can use the same app with their own connected data and permissions. We’re also making automations easier to add and manage so Sites can keep shared information up to date.\nL195: \nL196: Available to Business, Enterprise, Healthcare, and Edu plans. cite79†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L197: ### MCP events for plugin automations\nL198: \nL199: We’re adding support for the cite80†proposed MCP Events specification⁠(opens in a new window)†modelcontextprotocol.io , so plugins can start automations when something happens in a connected app. For example, you can ask ChatGPT to watch for new tasks on a project board. When one comes in, ChatGPT can read the linked documents and draft a plan, even while you’re away.\nL200: \nL201: Available to all plans. cite81†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L202: ### ChatGPT Space\nL203: \nL204: A new home for your team to collaborate with AI to get work done. Create a dedicated space where teammates, ChatGPT, and your dot can build on shared knowledge. ChatGPT can keep your space organized based on instructions you provide, so you can quickly find what you need and pick up where you left off.\nL205: Available to all Pro, Business, and Enterprise plans on the ChatGPT desktop app and web, with finding, reading, and sharing pages also available on mobile; mobile creation and editing are coming soon. cite82†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L206: ### Pages\nL207: \nL208: Pages are a new type of document, built for human and agent collaboration. Anything you can do in ChatGPT you can do on a page: write, research, generate charts, create images, or visualize information. Create a page in a conversation, then invite your team to contribute ideas and feedback.\nL209: \nL210: Available to all Pro, Business, and Enterprise plans. cite82†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L211: ### Collaborative slides\nL212: \nL213: Soon you’ll be able to create interactive slides with ChatGPT and your team, from a conversation or your own template. Multiple teammates and agents can edit the deck at the same time and leave comments. Present in ChatGPT or export to PowerPoint or Google Slides with formatting intact.\nL214: \nL215: Available to all Pro, Business, and Enterprise plans in the coming weeks. cite82†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L216: ### Create teams and share tasks\nL217: \nL218: Now you can bring colleagues together as a team in ChatGPT to share pages, slides, plugins, and spreadsheets. You can also delegate recurring work such as weekly project updates to team tasks. ChatGPT uses connected tools to gather information and take action on a schedule or in response to events like a new email or Slack message. Teammates can refine the instructions together as priorities change.\nL219: Available to all Business and Enterprise plans. cite83†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†learn.chatgpt.com L220: ### @ChatGPT in Slack and Microsoft Teams\nL221: \nL222: We’re making it easier to work with ChatGPT where team conversations happen. Mention @ChatGPT in a channel, thread or DM to get help with your team’s work. It can use tools connected by your admin or your own tools with your permissions. Teammates can add context and refine the results in the same conversation without needing an individual ChatGPT license.\nL223: \nL224: Available to all Business and Enterprise plans. cite84†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L225: ### Meetings plugin\nL226: \nL227: The Meetings plugin takes notes for you and saves personalized summaries with action items in ChatGPT Space. Keep them private or share them with your team, then ask ChatGPT to update a project plan or draft a follow-up. Audio is deleted once your notes are ready and can’t be accessed or replayed.\nL228: \nL229: Available in beta in the ChatGPT desktop app on macOS for all Pro and Business users, with Enterprise coming soon. cite85†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L230: ### Shareable profiles\nL231: \nL232: Shareable profiles let people showcase Sites and plugins that others can find and reuse. A profile brings those creations together so you can share them in one place. Teammates can also discover shared skills, with skills sharing limited to the workspace.\nL233: \nL234: Available to Free, Go, Plus, Pro, Business and Enterprise plans. cite86†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L235: ### Sign in with ChatGPT\nL236: \nL237: We’re making it easier to use your ChatGPT plan with other tools. Use your allowance across 16 partners including Cognition’s Devin, Notion, Vercel, T3, OpenClaw, and Dactyl, and control how much each can use. Eligible OpenAI usage counts toward your plan limits. You can also sign in to a range of tools with your ChatGPT account to connect plugins faster and manage fewer passwords.\nL238: Identity is available globally. Plan usage is available to Plus and Pro users in participating tools. cite87†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L239: ### A new Pro tier\nL240: \nL241: For those who want even more usage and speed, the new Pro 500 plan offers our highest usage allowance at 25 times the ChatGPT Plus allowance and includes access to Ultrafast.\nL242: \nL243: Available now. cite88†Learn more⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L244: ### OpenAI Marketplace\nL245: \nL246: Eligible enterprise customers can apply part of their existing OpenAI commitment toward approved partner software, giving them more flexibility in how they work with AI. Our first 32 partners include Figma for creative work; Adobe, Sierra, Decagon, Hubspot, Salesforce, and ServiceNow for customer experience; Harvey and Legora for legal; and Palo Alto Networks and CrowdStrike for cybersecurity and Baseten for open-source models.\nL247: Enterprise customers can express interest. cite89†Get started⁠ "
